13:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1b",null,{"guide":{"date":"March 23–24, 1862","place":"Sandy Ridge, Pritchard’s Hill, the Glass farm, and the Valley Turnpike south of Winchester, Virginia","result":"United States tactical victory. Nathan Kimball committed Erastus Tyler’s brigade and successive reinforcements against the Confederate line behind the stone wall on Sandy Ridge. Tyler’s compact assault column was stopped with heavy loss, but the United States troops recovered, extended the fight, and exhausted the defenders’ ammunition. Richard Garnett ordered a withdrawal shortly before dark without receiving an order from Jackson. United States troops took the wall and Jackson’s army escaped south. The attack nevertheless prompted the Lincoln administration to retain or redirect major forces toward the Shenandoah Valley and Washington instead of reinforcing McClellan on the Peninsula","facts":[{"label":"Episode scope","value":"The decisive Sandy Ridge fighting, Confederate retreat, immediate pursuit, casualty accounting, and strategic consequences of First Kernstown","note":"The episode resumes at roughly 3:45 p.m. on March 23 and follows events through the federal pursuit on March 24 and Washington’s reaction."},{"label":"Battle result","value":"United States victory","note":"The United States held the battlefield and Jackson withdrew south. NPS classifies Kernstown as a Union victory while also emphasizing the important Confederate strategic benefit."},{"label":"Strategic effect","value":"Federal troops were kept away from McClellan’s Peninsula operation","note":"Washington overestimated Jackson’s strength and treated the Valley as an active threat. Banks remained in the Valley, Alpheus Williams was recalled, Louis Blenker’s division was directed toward Frémont, and Irvin McDowell’s corps remained to cover Washington."},{"label":"Forces engaged","value":"About 7,200 United States troops and 3,700 Confederates","note":"NPS uses these engaged-strength figures in its battle narrative. Its order of battle gives larger approximate totals of 8,500 United States and 3,500 Confederate personnel. Jackson’s official report counted 2,742 infantry actually engaged, plus 18 guns and cavalry."},{"label":"Casualties","value":"590 United States; about 718–737 Confederate","note":"The episode totals the reports as 118 killed, 450 wounded, and 22 missing or captured for the United States; 139 killed, 312 wounded, 253 captured, and 33 missing for the Confederates, or 737. NPS sources give Confederate totals of about 718 or nearly 740. Categories and later consolidation of missing and captured men account for the variation."},{"label":"Confederate position","value":"A roughly 500-yard stone wall across a spur of Sandy Ridge","note":"The 27th Virginia first occupied the sector. Reinforcements extended the defense, using the wall and the rising ground behind it as cover. Map positions and frontages remain approximate."},{"label":"Tyler’s formation","value":"Close column of divisions, about 75 yards wide and roughly 400 yards deep","note":"The five-regiment brigade advanced with only two companies in front and the remaining companies stacked behind. Woods and combat prevented an orderly deployment before Confederate fire struck the head of the column."},{"label":"Tyler’s brigade","value":"7th Ohio, 7th Indiana, 1st Virginia (later West Virginia), 110th Pennsylvania, and 29th Ohio","note":"Eight companies of United States cavalry under Lt. Col. Joseph T. Copeland followed the infantry."},{"label":"Fight opens on Sandy Ridge","value":"About 4 p.m.","note":"The 27th Virginia skirmishers withdrew behind the stone wall and fired into the head of Tyler’s column at less than 100 yards."},{"label":"Initial United States setback","value":"Tyler’s leading regiments lost cohesion within minutes","note":"The 7th Ohio and 7th Indiana took cover; the 110th Pennsylvania broke to the rear; and its flight divided the 29th Ohio. The 1st Virginia maintained better order but its flank attack was repulsed."},{"label":"Thoburn wounded","value":"Col. Joseph Thoburn suffered a shattered upper arm","note":"Fulkerson’s 23rd and 37th Virginia reached the previously open western section of wall just before Thoburn’s 1st Virginia. Their close-range volley stopped the attack. Thoburn survived."},{"label":"Confederate line at 4:30 p.m.","value":"About 1,200 men behind the wall","note":"The episode, following Gary Ecelbarger, describes the 27th, 21st, 33rd, 4th, and parts of other commands forming a defensive line while Tyler’s mixed units fought from woods, a ravine, and open ground to the north."},{"label":"Kimball reinforces","value":"Elements of five more regiments entered the Sandy Ridge fight","note":"Four companies of the 8th Ohio were followed by the 67th Ohio, 84th Pennsylvania, part of the 5th Ohio, and finally the 14th Indiana. The attacks were piecemeal, but they added pressure the smaller Confederate force could not sustain indefinitely."},{"label":"Colonel Murray killed","value":"Col. William G. Murray of the 84th Pennsylvania was killed","note":"Murray was shot in the head during his regiment’s third advance toward the wall. His death helped break the regiment’s attack."},{"label":"14th Indiana charge","value":"The regiment crossed the open ground and reached the wall about 6 p.m.","note":"The episode credits Pvt. Paul Truckey of Company G with starting the forward rush. The automated transcript renders his surname phonetically as “Truckee.”"},{"label":"Confederate ammunition crisis","value":"Several regiments had exhausted or nearly exhausted their cartridges","note":"The 21st Virginia had only scavenged rounds left and the 27th Virginia had none when the withdrawal order arrived. Casualties, exhaustion, straggling, and concern about cavalry beyond the left compounded the problem."},{"label":"Garnett’s decision","value":"Ordered withdrawal from the stone wall without an order from Jackson","note":"Garnett had no current information about Jackson’s plan or the reserves approaching from the rear. Given the ammunition shortage, thinning line, and threat to the left, the retreat preserved much of the command. Jackson later treated the decision as disobedience and ordered Garnett’s arrest."},{"label":"Command breakdown","value":"Jackson and Garnett did not meet during the main two-hour fight","note":"The episode places Jackson near the Rockbridge Artillery, roughly a quarter mile from the wall. Garnett was not told Jackson’s location, reserve movements, or full intent. Jackson reached Garnett only after the retreat was underway."},{"label":"Captured gun","value":"One Rockbridge Artillery piece was left behind","note":"Lt. William T. Poague brought two guns forward during the retreat. United States pressure forced his section back so quickly that one piece could not be removed."},{"label":"Rear guard","value":"The 5th and 42nd Virginia covered the escape","note":"They briefly stabilized the position after sunset. The 13th Indiana and other United States units continued the advance, but darkness and exhaustion limited pursuit."},{"label":"Aide captured","value":"Lt. George Junkin was captured during the retreat","note":"The episode credits Privates Lee Frazier and Bob Owens of the 14th Indiana. Junkin was a member of Jackson’s staff, not a battlefield commander."},{"label":"Night pursuit","value":"Kimball halted until morning","note":"He cited exhaustion and lack of food and water. The pursuit resumed at first light on March 24 and encountered Ashby’s rear guard near Middletown that afternoon."},{"label":"Book recommendation","value":"“Decoying the Yanks: Jackson’s Valley Campaign” by Champ Clark and the Editors of Time-Life Books"}],"maps":[{"title":"About 4 p.m.: Tyler’s column strikes the stone wall","caption":"Tyler advanced south through the woods in a close column only about two companies wide. Confederate skirmishers fell back to the east–west stone wall. The 27th Virginia stopped the head of the column while the 21st Virginia, Fulkerson’s brigade, and additional Stonewall Brigade regiments filled the line. The diagram is schematic: routes, distances, vegetation, and unit frontages are approximate.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/first-battle-of-kernstown.htm","image":"/images/ep143-sandy-ridge-assault.svg","alt":"Schematic map of Tyler’s close column advancing south on Sandy Ridge toward the Confederate stone-wall position about 4 p.m."},{"title":"About 6 p.m.: the wall breaks and Jackson retreats","caption":"The 14th Indiana reached the eastern end of the line as Confederate ammunition ran out. Garnett ordered the withdrawal; some units did not receive the order and were exposed to enfilading fire and cavalry near the Glass farm. Jackson’s late-arriving 5th and 42nd Virginia covered the escape toward the Valley Turnpike. This phase map is schematic and does not show exact paths, timing, or capture locations.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/first-battle-of-kernstown.htm","image":"/images/ep143-sandy-ridge-retreat.svg","alt":"Schematic map of the Confederate withdrawal from Sandy Ridge after the United States advance reached the stone wall near 6 p.m."}],"moments":[{"time":"0:49","audioTime":49,"title":"A United States veteran describes the approach","detail":"The opening account follows the 14th Indiana past wounded men of the 84th Pennsylvania and 5th Ohio toward the stone wall. The regiment’s charge occurs later in the episode."},{"time":"3:24","audioTime":204,"title":"A 2nd Virginia veteran recalls the retreat","detail":"Sgt. William B. Colston describes crossing open ground, fighting until wounded, and escaping on Maj. Lawson Botts’s horse after the order to fall back."},{"time":"6:22","audioTime":382,"title":"The narrative resumes after Pendleton’s warning","detail":"Jackson now understood that the force between him and Winchester was much larger than Ashby’s original estimate."},{"time":"7:44","audioTime":464,"title":"Jackson abandons the planned turning movement","detail":"He shifted from trying to outflank Pritchard’s Hill to protecting the Confederate artillery on Sandy Ridge as five United States regimental flags approached."},{"time":"8:05","audioTime":485,"title":"The 27th Virginia moves beyond the guns","detail":"Jackson bypassed Garnett and directly ordered Col. John Echols to advance. The regiment moved about 800 yards to the stone fence and then into open timber beyond it."},{"time":"9:03","audioTime":543,"title":"The 27th Virginia is in position by about 3:45 p.m.","detail":"Echols sent an officer to inform Garnett that Jackson had moved the regiment. The stone wall ran roughly east–west across a low spur of Sandy Ridge."},{"time":"9:28","audioTime":568,"title":"Kimball orders Tyler to clear Sandy Ridge","detail":"After recognizing the western threat, Kimball sent Tyler’s five infantry regiments and eight cavalry companies toward the Confederate flank."},{"time":"12:49","audioTime":769,"title":"Tyler forms a deep assault column","detail":"The brigade advanced with a front of about two companies and a depth of 24 lines. The formation made control easier in woods but left most of the brigade unable to fire forward."},{"time":"14:27","audioTime":867,"title":"Skirmishers collide before 4 p.m.","detail":"United States skirmishers met Company G of the 27th Virginia about 300 yards north of the wall. The Confederate skirmishers withdrew as Tyler’s column continued."},{"time":"16:32","audioTime":992,"title":"The wall opens fire at close range","detail":"At about 4 p.m., with Tyler’s leading troops less than 100 yards away, the 27th Virginia fired. The 7th Ohio lost cohesion while officers tried to deploy it."},{"time":"18:30","audioTime":1110,"title":"Tyler’s column fragments","detail":"The 7th Indiana also took cover, the 110th Pennsylvania broke, and the flight divided the 29th Ohio. The 1st Virginia remained relatively organized."},{"time":"19:45","audioTime":1185,"title":"Jackson sends the 21st Virginia to the wall","detail":"Again bypassing a brigade commander, Jackson ordered John M. Patton Jr. forward. The regiment formed to the right of the 27th Virginia."},{"time":"20:08","audioTime":1208,"title":"Thoburn attempts to turn the Confederate left","detail":"The 1st Virginia moved toward an unoccupied western section of wall, but Fulkerson’s 23rd and 37th Virginia arrived first and stopped the charge. Thoburn was wounded."},{"time":"22:58","audioTime":1378,"title":"Garnett extends the Confederate defense","detail":"Within about 15 minutes of the first volleys, he brought the 33rd Virginia and then the 4th Virginia into the line."},{"time":"24:02","audioTime":1442,"title":"The fight reaches a temporary stalemate","detail":"By about 4:30 p.m., roughly 1,200 Confederates held the wall. Tyler still had larger numbers nearby, but his brigade was mixed, scattered, and unable to conduct a coordinated assault."},{"time":"25:05","audioTime":1505,"title":"Jackson lengthens the line and keeps reserves","detail":"The 2nd Virginia and Irish Battalion entered the fight. Jackson still held the 5th, 42nd, and 48th Virginia behind the front and expected darkness to end the action."},{"time":"27:23","audioTime":1643,"title":"Kimball begins piecemeal reinforcement","detail":"Four companies of the 8th Ohio advanced first, followed by the 67th Ohio, 84th Pennsylvania, and part of the 5th Ohio from Pritchard’s Hill."},{"time":"29:28","audioTime":1768,"title":"Colonel Murray is killed","detail":"The 84th Pennsylvania reached within about 40 yards of the wall on its third attempt. William G. Murray was shot in the head and the regiment broke."},{"time":"30:05","audioTime":1805,"title":"Nine United States regiments are engaged around Sandy Ridge","detail":"The federal units lacked a single coherent line, but their cumulative pressure forced the Confederates to expend limited ammunition across a long front."},{"time":"30:17","audioTime":1817,"title":"Kimball commits the 14th Indiana at 5:30 p.m.","detail":"His former regiment advanced through the woods, crossed into a small clearing, and exchanged close-range fire with the Confederates behind the wall."},{"time":"31:44","audioTime":1904,"title":"Paul Truckey starts the charge","detail":"The 14th Indiana followed the private’s rush toward the stone wall. His name is mistranscribed as “Truckee” in the automated transcript."},{"time":"32:46","audioTime":1966,"title":"Patton withdraws the 21st Virginia","detail":"At about 6 p.m., with ammunition gone, Patton received Garnett’s order and left the right end of the Confederate line as the 14th Indiana came over the wall."},{"time":"34:55","audioTime":2095,"title":"Garnett orders a general withdrawal","detail":"Casualties, empty cartridge boxes, men leaving the ranks, and reports of cavalry beyond the left convinced him that remaining at the wall risked envelopment and capture."},{"time":"36:11","audioTime":2171,"title":"United States fire catches units left behind","detail":"Part of the 4th Virginia and Fulkerson’s regiments had not received the withdrawal order. Enfilading fire drove many west toward the Glass farm and United States cavalry."},{"time":"37:52","audioTime":2272,"title":"Jackson’s reserves are still approaching","detail":"The 5th, 42nd, and 48th Virginia had been ordered forward, but Garnett did not know they were coming and Jackson did not yet understand that the wall had broken."},{"time":"38:14","audioTime":2294,"title":"Jackson discovers the retreat","detail":"Men of the 21st Virginia streamed past the Rockbridge Artillery after exhausting their ammunition. Jackson told one man to return and use the bayonet."},{"time":"40:25","audioTime":2425,"title":"Jackson and Garnett finally meet","detail":"Jackson mistook the 5th Virginia’s proposed rear-guard position for another retreat movement and confronted Garnett. He then ordered the regiment forward."},{"time":"41:10","audioTime":2470,"title":"Poague loses a gun","detail":"Two Rockbridge Artillery pieces advanced into the federal pursuit. The section fired canister but had to retire, leaving one cannon behind."},{"time":"41:53","audioTime":2513,"title":"The rear guard forms after sunset","detail":"The 5th Virginia fell back to the position Garnett had selected. The 42nd Virginia arrived on its right, and both units held long enough for the rest of the army to escape."},{"time":"43:05","audioTime":2585,"title":"The 13th Indiana renews the United States advance","detail":"Sullivan’s veteran regiment came through the smoke, but darkness and Confederate rear-guard action limited the pursuit."},{"time":"43:33","audioTime":2613,"title":"George Junkin is captured","detail":"Two soldiers of the 14th Indiana overtook Jackson’s aide during the withdrawal."},{"time":"46:16","audioTime":2776,"title":"Kimball halts for the night","detail":"The United States commander judged his troops too exhausted and hungry for a night pursuit. Soldiers instead searched the field for wounded comrades."},{"time":"47:15","audioTime":2835,"title":"The episode gives casualty totals","detail":"It reports 590 United States casualties and 737 Confederates killed, wounded, captured, or missing. Modern NPS summaries consolidate the Confederate total differently."},{"time":"47:38","audioTime":2858,"title":"Banks returns and pursuit resumes","detail":"Kimball moved at first light on March 24. United States cavalry did not reach Ashby’s rear guard near Middletown until about 1:30 p.m."},{"time":"48:11","audioTime":2891,"title":"Shields claims public credit","detail":"His report presented Kimball as an executor of Shields’s orders. Northern newspapers repeated that framing even though Kimball had exercised battlefield command."},{"time":"49:30","audioTime":2970,"title":"The episode assesses Jackson’s errors","detail":"Jackson accepted bad intelligence, failed to confer with Ashby, withheld his plan and location from subordinates, and stayed away from the stone-wall line during the main fight."},{"time":"50:40","audioTime":3040,"title":"Tactical defeat produces a strategic diversion","detail":"Jackson acknowledged losing the field but argued that the attack had recalled troops to the Valley and prevented their concentration elsewhere."},{"time":"51:57","audioTime":3117,"title":"Lincoln redirects Blenker’s division","detail":"The approximately 10,000-man division was detached from McClellan and directed toward Frémont, with instructions connected to the Valley threat."},{"time":"52:46","audioTime":3166,"title":"McDowell remains to cover Washington","detail":"His roughly 40,000-man corps did not sail for the Peninsula, reducing the force available to McClellan’s move on Richmond."},{"time":"53:48","audioTime":3228,"title":"The hosts recommend “Decoying the Yanks”","detail":"The episode closes with Champ Clark and the Editors of Time-Life Books as its recommended introduction to Jackson’s Valley Campaign."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Nathaniel P. Banks","command":"V Corps, Army of the Potomac","subordinates":"Absent from the field during the battle; returned to Winchester on March 24 and joined the pursuit"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. James Shields","command":"Second Division, V Corps","subordinates":"Recovering from his March 22 wound in Winchester; received reports but did not exercise direct battlefield control"},{"commander":"Col. Nathan Kimball","command":"First Brigade and acting field commander","subordinates":"14th Indiana, 8th Ohio, 67th Ohio, and 84th Pennsylvania; directed the division in the field and sent successive reinforcements to Sandy Ridge"},{"commander":"Col. Jeremiah C. Sullivan","command":"Second Brigade","subordinates":"5th Ohio, 13th Indiana, 62nd Ohio, and unengaged 39th Illinois; the 5th Ohio and 13th Indiana entered the Sandy Ridge action"},{"commander":"Col. Erastus B. Tyler","command":"Third Brigade","subordinates":"7th Ohio, 7th Indiana, 1st Virginia (later West Virginia), 29th Ohio, and 110th Pennsylvania; attacked Sandy Ridge in close column"},{"commander":"Lt. Col. Joseph T. Copeland","command":"Cavalry detachment with Tyler","subordinates":"Eight companies followed Tyler’s infantry; other United States cavalry operated near the Glass farm and took prisoners during the Confederate retreat"},{"commander":"Lt. Col. Philip Daum","command":"Division artillery","subordinates":"United States and Ohio batteries on Pritchard’s Hill continued supporting the Sandy Ridge attacks"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#9a3d32","branches":[{"commander":"Gen. Joseph E. Johnston","command":"Department of Northern Virginia","subordinates":"Strategic superior who had directed Jackson to prevent United States troops from leaving the Valley"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson","command":"Valley District","subordinates":"Directed the infantry brigades, Ashby’s cavalry, and artillery; remained near the artillery during the main wall fight and ordered late reserves forward"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Richard B. Garnett","command":"Stonewall Brigade","subordinates":"2nd, 4th, 5th, 27th, and 33rd Virginia Infantry; managed the main line at the wall and ordered the withdrawal when ammunition and cohesion failed"},{"commander":"Col. Jesse S. Burks","command":"Second Brigade","subordinates":"21st, 42nd, and 48th Virginia Infantry and 1st Virginia (Irish) Battalion; Jackson directly ordered some of these units during the fight"},{"commander":"Col. Samuel V. Fulkerson","command":"Third Brigade","subordinates":"23rd and 37th Virginia Infantry; filled the western section of the wall and was partly cut off when the withdrawal order failed to reach the entire line"},{"commander":"Col. Turner Ashby","command":"7th Virginia Cavalry and attached forces","subordinates":"Operated east of the Valley Turnpike during the battle and covered the retreat toward Middletown on March 24"},{"commander":"Capt. William N. Pendleton / Lt. William T. Poague","command":"Rockbridge Artillery","subordinates":"Part of Jackson’s artillery position on Sandy Ridge; Poague’s forward two-gun section lost one piece during the retreat"}]}],"people":[{"name":"Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson","role":"Confederate commander whose late reserve movement could not prevent the collapse of the stone-wall line; his attack caused major United States strategic redeployments","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/ep143-stonewall-jackson.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2003674500/"},{"name":"Nathan Kimball","role":"United States field commander who sent Tyler to Sandy Ridge, reinforced the stalled attack, held the battlefield, and resumed pursuit on March 24","side":"United States"},{"name":"Erastus B. Tyler","role":"Third Brigade commander whose close-column assault was stopped in confusion before his mixed units recovered and maintained pressure on the wall","side":"United States","image":"/images/ep143-erastus-tyler.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2022630733/"},{"name":"Richard B. Garnett","role":"Stonewall Brigade commander who controlled the wall defense and ordered a necessary withdrawal without Jackson’s authorization","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Samuel V. Fulkerson","role":"Commanded the 23rd and 37th Virginia, stopped Thoburn’s flank attack, and withdrew after part of the Confederate line had already left","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"John Echols","role":"Colonel of the 27th Virginia who placed skirmishers north of the wall and then opened the main infantry fight at close range","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"John M. Patton Jr.","role":"Lieutenant colonel of the 21st Virginia who held the right of the wall until ammunition was exhausted and Garnett ordered him back","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Joseph Thoburn","role":"Colonel of the 1st Virginia who attempted to take the open western section of wall and was wounded when Fulkerson arrived first","side":"United States"},{"name":"William G. Murray","role":"Colonel of the 84th Pennsylvania killed while leading the regiment’s third advance toward the stone wall","side":"United States"},{"name":"John H. Patrick","role":"Lieutenant colonel of the 5th Ohio who continued forward with only part of his regiment after a recall order failed to reach the leading companies","side":"United States"},{"name":"Franklin Sawyer","role":"Lieutenant colonel who led four companies of the 8th Ohio into Kimball’s first reinforcement of Tyler","side":"United States"},{"name":"Paul Truckey","role":"Private in the 14th Indiana credited with initiating the regiment’s final rush toward the wall","side":"United States"},{"name":"William T. Poague","role":"Rockbridge Artillery lieutenant whose two-gun section fired canister during the retreat and left one gun behind","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"William H. Harman","role":"Colonel of the 5th Virginia who formed a rear-guard position south of the wall as United States troops pressed forward after sunset","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Jesse S. Burks","role":"Confederate brigade commander whose 21st, 42nd, and Irish Battalion fought on Sandy Ridge, although Jackson often issued orders directly to regimental officers","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Jeremiah C. Sullivan","role":"United States brigade commander whose 5th Ohio and 13th Indiana reinforced the attack and pursuit","side":"United States"},{"name":"George Junkin","role":"Jackson staff officer captured by soldiers of the 14th Indiana during the Confederate retreat","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"James Shields","role":"Wounded United States division commander who remained in Winchester and later framed the victory in his official report as the execution of his orders","side":"United States"},{"name":"Nathaniel P. Banks","role":"V Corps commander absent during the battle who returned on March 24 and supported the pursuit of Jackson","side":"United States"},{"name":"Abraham Lincoln","role":"President who reacted to the Kernstown threat by retaining McDowell near Washington and redirecting additional troops toward the Valley and Frémont","side":"United States"}],"losses":[{"name":"Col. William G. Murray","side":"United States","role":"Commander, 84th Pennsylvania Infantry","outcome":"Killed instantly by a shot to the head while leading the regiment’s third advance to within about 40 yards of the stone wall"}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/ep143-erastus-tyler.jpg","alt":"Wartime carte-de-visite portrait of United States officer Erastus B. Tyler in uniform","caption":"Erastus B. Tyler led the five-regiment brigade sent to clear Sandy Ridge. His choice of a deep column delayed deployment and exposed the leading regiments to concentrated fire from the stone wall.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2022630733/"},{"image":"/images/ep143-stonewall-jackson.jpg","alt":"Wartime photographic portrait of Confederate general Thomas J. Stonewall Jackson in uniform","caption":"Jackson remained near the Sandy Ridge artillery during the main infantry fight. He did not learn that Garnett’s ammunition-starved line was withdrawing until after 6 p.m.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2003674500/"},{"image":"/images/ep143-kernstown-sneden.jpg","alt":"Robert Knox Sneden’s color plan of the Battle of Kernstown showing roads, terrain, and troop positions","caption":"Robert Knox Sneden’s wartime-era plan helps locate Sandy Ridge relative to Pritchard’s Hill and the Valley Turnpike. It is a historical interpretation rather than a surveyed map, and its troop-strength annotation is much higher than official reports and modern estimates.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/gvhs01.vhs00077/"},{"image":"/images/ep143-sandy-ridge-assault.svg","alt":"Schematic map of the close-column United States assault against the Confederate stone-wall line on Sandy Ridge","caption":"Tyler’s narrow column entered wooded ground and met the 27th Virginia at the wall before it could form an effective line. Confederate reinforcements arrived quickly enough to stop the first flank attempt.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/first-battle-of-kernstown.htm"},{"image":"/images/ep143-sandy-ridge-retreat.svg","alt":"Schematic map of Garnett’s withdrawal and the Confederate rear guard on Sandy Ridge","caption":"After about two hours of close fighting, empty cartridge boxes and pressure on both ends of the line forced Garnett to withdraw. Late reserves covered a disordered escape after sunset.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/first-battle-of-kernstown.htm"}],"sources":[{"label":"Official Civil War Podcast episode 143 page","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2016/02/21/civil-war-podcast-episode-143/"},{"label":"National Park Service: First Battle of Kernstown","url":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/first-battle-of-kernstown.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Kernstown I battle detail","url":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=va101"},{"label":"National Park Service: Jackson’s 1862 Shenandoah Valley Campaign","url":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/jackson1862-valley-campaign.htm"},{"label":"U.S. Army Center of Military History: The Virginia Campaigns, March–August 1862","url":"https://history.army.mil/portals/143/Images/Publications/catalog/75-5.pdf"},{"label":"Primary source collection: Official Records, Series I, Volume XII, Part I","url":"https://ehistory.osu.edu/books/official-records/015"},{"label":"Primary source: Shields’s report and United States casualty return, Official Records page 342","url":"https://ehistory.osu.edu/books/official-records/015/0342"},{"label":"Primary source: Jackson’s report and Confederate strength, Official Records page 383","url":"https://ehistory.osu.edu/books/official-records/015/0383"},{"label":"Primary source: Nathan Kimball, “Fighting Jackson at Kernstown,” Battles and Leaders, volume II","url":"https://ehistory.osu.edu/books/battles/vol2/302"},{"label":"Shenandoah Valley Battlefields National Historic District: First Kernstown","url":"https://www.shenandoahatwar.org/first-kernstown-article"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Robert Knox Sneden plan of the Battle of Kernstown","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/gvhs01.vhs00077/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Erastus B. Tyler portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2022630733/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2003674500/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Hotchkiss map collection history","url":"https://www.loc.gov/collections/hotchkiss-maps/articles-and-essays/the-hotchkiss-collection-of-confederate-maps/"},{"label":"Official podcast book-recommendations list","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/book-recommendations/"}]}}]}]
14:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section","id":"people","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"People and command"}],[[["$","link","css:/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/page.m4AAmS8w.css",{"rel":"stylesheet","precedence":"vite-rsc/importer-resources","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/page.m4AAmS8w.css","data-rsc-css-href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/page.m4AAmS8w.css"}],["$","link","css:/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/index.B0pjVDS7.css",{"rel":"stylesheet","precedence":"vite-rsc/importer-resources","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/index.B0pjVDS7.css","data-rsc-css-href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/index.B0pjVDS7.css"}],["$","link","css:/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/command-trees.KCNQiAeH.css",{"rel":"stylesheet","precedence":"vite-rsc/importer-resources","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/command-trees.KCNQiAeH.css","data-rsc-css-href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/command-trees.KCNQiAeH.css"}],"$undefined"],[["$","div",null,{"className":"command-grid reference-command","children":[["$","section","United States",{"className":"command-column #315f8c","children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":"United States"}],[["$","div","V Corps, Army of the Potomac-Maj. Gen. Nathaniel P. Banks",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"V Corps, Army of the Potomac"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Maj. Gen. Nathaniel P. Banks"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Absent from the field during the battle; returned to Winchester on March 24 and joined the pursuit"}]]}],["$","div","Second Division, V Corps-Brig. Gen. James Shields",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Second Division, V Corps"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Brig. Gen. James Shields"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Recovering from his March 22 wound in Winchester; received reports but did not exercise direct battlefield control"}]]}],["$","div","First Brigade and acting field commander-Col. Nathan Kimball",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"First Brigade and acting field commander"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Col. Nathan Kimball"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"14th Indiana, 8th Ohio, 67th Ohio, and 84th Pennsylvania; directed the division in the field and sent successive reinforcements to Sandy Ridge"}]]}],["$","div","Second Brigade-Col. Jeremiah C. Sullivan",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Second Brigade"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Col. Jeremiah C. Sullivan"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"5th Ohio, 13th Indiana, 62nd Ohio, and unengaged 39th Illinois; the 5th Ohio and 13th Indiana entered the Sandy Ridge action"}]]}],["$","div","Third Brigade-Col. Erastus B. Tyler",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Third Brigade"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Col. Erastus B. Tyler"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"7th Ohio, 7th Indiana, 1st Virginia (later West Virginia), 29th Ohio, and 110th Pennsylvania; attacked Sandy Ridge in close column"}]]}],["$","div","Cavalry detachment with Tyler-Lt. Col. Joseph T. Copeland",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Cavalry detachment with Tyler"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Lt. Col. Joseph T. Copeland"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Eight companies followed Tyler’s infantry; other United States cavalry operated near the Glass farm and took prisoners during the Confederate retreat"}]]}],["$","div","Division artillery-Lt. Col. Philip Daum",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Division artillery"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Lt. Col. Philip Daum"}],"$L1c"]}]]]}],"$L1d"]}],"$L1e"]]]}]
15:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section","id":"losses","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Important leaders killed or wounded"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"loss-list","children":[["$","article","Col. William G. Murray",{"children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"side union","children":"United States"}],["$","h3",null,{"children":"Col. William G. Murray"}],["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":["Commander, 84th Pennsylvania Infantry","."]}]," ","Killed instantly by a shot to the head while leading the regiment’s third advance to within about 40 yards of the stone wall"]}]]}]]}]]}]
16:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section","id":"images","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Historical images"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"history-gallery","children":[["$","$L1f","/images/ep143-erastus-tyler.jpg",{"image":"/images/ep143-erastus-tyler.jpg","alt":"Wartime carte-de-visite portrait of United States officer Erastus B. Tyler in uniform","caption":"Erastus B. Tyler led the five-regiment brigade sent to clear Sandy Ridge. His choice of a deep column delayed deployment and exposed the leading regiments to concentrated fire from the stone wall.","name":"Wartime carte-de-visite portrait of United States officer Erastus B. Tyler in uniform","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2022630733/","credit":"$undefined"}],["$","$L1f","/images/ep143-stonewall-jackson.jpg",{"image":"/images/ep143-stonewall-jackson.jpg","alt":"Wartime photographic portrait of Confederate general Thomas J. Stonewall Jackson in uniform","caption":"Jackson remained near the Sandy Ridge artillery during the main infantry fight. He did not learn that Garnett’s ammunition-starved line was withdrawing until after 6 p.m.","name":"Wartime photographic portrait of Confederate general Thomas J. Stonewall Jackson in uniform","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2003674500/","credit":"$undefined"}],["$","$L1f","/images/ep143-kernstown-sneden.jpg",{"image":"/images/ep143-kernstown-sneden.jpg","alt":"Robert Knox Sneden’s color plan of the Battle of Kernstown showing roads, terrain, and troop positions","caption":"Robert Knox Sneden’s wartime-era plan helps locate Sandy Ridge relative to Pritchard’s Hill and the Valley Turnpike. It is a historical interpretation rather than a surveyed map, and its troop-strength annotation is much higher than official reports and modern estimates.","name":"Robert Knox Sneden’s color plan of the Battle of Kernstown showing roads, terrain, and troop positions","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/gvhs01.vhs00077/","credit":"$undefined"}]]}]]}]
